# Generated and downloaded data. None of it belongs in the repository: the FASTA files are
# reproduced by the goal `extractrefseqcsv' from the project's configuration, and everything under
# data/common is downloaded from the NCBI. Both run to tens of thousands of files and many
# gigabytes, and both are derived from what *is* tracked -- the taxids, categories and config files
# of each project.

# Per-accession FASTA files written by `extractrefseqcsv' (through the goal extractrefseqfasta).
/data/projects/*/fasta/

# Simulated and downloaded reads, together with the fastq mapping files that make_fastqs.sh writes
# next to them. Those maps are generated from the reads they point at and hold absolute paths, so
# they are valid only on the machine that produced them.
/data/fastq/*
# The accession list of the real Nanopore tick runs is input, not generated.
!/data/fastq/seventicks.txt
# So is the map that declares those runs by URL for the goal `fastqdownload'. It holds URLs rather
# than absolute paths, so unlike the generated maps above it is valid on any machine.
!/data/fastq/ticks_real.txt
# ... and the same for the human saliva runs the first paper used.
!/data/fastq/saliva_real.txt
# Single-sample maps, written by hand to score one file at a time. Like the two above they name
# files rather than absolute paths, so they are valid anywhere.
!/data/fastq/ticks_sim_tick1.txt
!/data/fastq/saliva_mate1.txt

# RefSeq and Genbank archives, the taxonomy dumps and the assembly summaries.
/data/common/*
# Re-include the directory itself before excluding its contents, since git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded.
!/data/common/fasta/
/data/common/fasta/*
# The list of extra genomes to download is project setup, not downloaded data.
!/data/common/fasta/downloads.txt
